00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.720
- And this might be an obvious question,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.710
but I think it's worth asking.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.770
So the Constitution actually,

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.380
this wasn't obvious to me
when I first learned it,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:30.687
you know, you just learned
the word Constitution.

00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:32.530
And then like, literally I was in my 30's

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.190
one day when I woke up in
the morning I was like,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.050
Oh, because it constitutes the country.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.660
It like says what the country is made of.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.400
How does it run, et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.290
I never made that connection
like my entire life

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.810
until I was like 38 years old.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.530
But why is it important?

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.680
Obviously it's important
to have a Constitution.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.540
But why is it important
to keep reflecting on it

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.650
and studying it, you know,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:53.970
to have students be able to
talk to Supreme Court Justices,

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.510
to have a museum around this?

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:56.530
Why is that important to keep

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.900
reflecting on the Constitution?

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.490
- You know, you're so right
to emphasize that word

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:06.160
constitute because a Constitution
is what constitutes us

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.290
as a people, as we the people.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:10.840
And those words of the preamble,

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.290
we the people of the United States

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:17.010
were hugely significant
of the framer James Wilson

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.190
insisted that the preamble begin with

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.240
we the people of United
States not his original draft.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.290
We the people of the
States of Rhode Island

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.650
and Virginia and so forth

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.900
because we believe that
we the national people

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:29.350
have the sovereign power.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:32.950
It wasn't like England
where the King had the power

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:36.070
or where parliament have the power.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.760
It was we the people as
a whole who had the power

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.200
and we all know friends that
these are such divided times.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.820
According to some scholars
were more polarized

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.800
than at any time since the civil war.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.860
The text of the Constitution
is the one thing

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.370
that continues to bind and constitute us.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.440
And it doesn't mean that we agree about

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.610
what it means always,

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.450
but we can agree and disagree respectfully

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:02.910
on the basis of our Constitutional
not our political views.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.497
And in that sense,
studying the Constitution

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.350
and the various arguments on all sides

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:07.970
of Constitutional questions,

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.310
helps us to have the civil dialogue

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.300
that the framers thought was necessary,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.770
if we are continued to be
constituted as we the people.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.680
- And even what you just
pointed out about how,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.840
you know, early revisions had

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.620
we the people of the States of

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.340
versus obviously the Constitution we have,

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.880
which is we the people
of the United States,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.170
you know, we take even words
like United for granted.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.600
That's just the name of
our country, United States.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.880
But that was a really important point

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.000
because you could have
constituted as essentially of

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.000
confederation of Quasi-independent
states or nations

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.010
or however you wanna talk about it.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:45.140
And that's obviously what the articles

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.420
of confederation world we're all about.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.530
But then they said, well
that's too federated.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.343
We're gonna be one people.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:54.690
- That's exactly right.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.960
And as you say, Sal was that

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.800
defects of the articles of confederation

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.940
that led those guys to come
to Philadelphia in 1787.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.300
The Articles of Confederation

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.160
viewed each state as sovereign.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:07.240
And in order to do anything

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.040
you had to have unanimous consent.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.370
So it was impossible to
pay off the war debts,

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:14.540
to have foreign policy,

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.170
individual states for raising
tariffs against each other.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.690
So the framers come to Philadelphia

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.670
with this very difficult task.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.600
They have to create a central
government, strong enough

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.660
to protect common purposes,

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:28.460
but constrained enough to protect liberty.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:31.990
And it could have gone lots of ways.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:32.823
The very,

00:07:32.823 --> 00:07:34.980
it's so cool, if you go on
The Interactive Constitution,

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.150
you can see the early
drafts of The Preamble

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.500
and the very first words
that Wilson chose weren't,

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.220
we the people, it was just resolve

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.880
the government of the United States

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.010
shall consist of a legislative, judicial

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:46.360
and executive branch.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.193
That was it.

00:07:47.193 --> 00:07:50.350
Those three branches of
government were the big idea.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.860
But note, then after
further debate it was clear.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.530
The branches don't have
the sovereign power.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.050
None of them can speak for us.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:58.450
We the people are sovereign.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.900
But then, the next profound question,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.020
which people are sovereign?

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:03.650
Is it the people of the individual states

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:04.950
or the sovereign states?

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.760
Now in insisting that the
United States were sovereign

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.710
and that the style of the United States,

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.070
according to the second graph

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.010
shall be the United States of America.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.180
Wilson was introducing a radical idea,

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.030
James Wilson or rather Abraham Lincoln.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.120
When the south tried to
succeed from the union

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.020
during the civil war,

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:26.210
invoked James Wilson's idea of
national popular sovereignty

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.480
and said, you can't do
that, Southern States.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.310
We the people of the
United States as a whole

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:31.450
having made the union,

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:34.530
we need the consent of the
entire people to change it.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.420
And it took the civil war
to settle that question.

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:39.990
So look at how much we've reflected on.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:41.580
And we haven't even gotten past

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.300
the first three words of the Constitution.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:48.210
Just by digging into the text,
the history, the case law,

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.420
we can learn so much
about the evolving notion

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.720
of who we are as a people.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:54.603
- That was very powerful.

00:08:54.603 --> 00:08:56.390
And actually another thing
which you touched on,

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:57.930
but, you know, these are
these things that I had

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.520
like these epiphanies in
my 30s and I was like,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:01.500
Oh, of course, you know,
in the United States,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.100
we call each state a state because

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:06.050
they were initially
envisioned to be like a state.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.600
Like a Quasi-independent state.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:10.980
In most countries you
call these provinces.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.500
You don't call them states.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.010
But we've kept that even though

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.483
we really are a part of one country.

