00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:26.430
frankly I wanna learn more,

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.660
but also for my family
to engage with that.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:29.850
I think that's incredible.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:31.730
You know, going into
the Constitution itself.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:33.010
And obviously people could,

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:34.430
I actually highly recommend people

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:35.930
to look at the text of the Constitution.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.610
I think most people watching

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.110
are fully capable of reading it.

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:40.220
You don't have to be a lawyer.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.350
You don't have to be a historian.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:43.180
It is quite approachable.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:44.220
But you know, one of the things

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:45.640
that all has always fascinated me

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.740
is this notion of Amendments,

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.480
you know, and we have the bill of rights

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:50.680
for first 10 Amendments.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.070
The original authors of the Constitution,

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:55.470
how much did they think

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.130
it was going to be an evolving document?

00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:59.580
I mean, I guess they started
off kind of out the gate,

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:01.730
evolving it a little bit
with the bill of rights.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.940
So and do you think they
would have been surprised

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:04.920
that we haven't amended it more?

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:05.770
Or they would have been surprised

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:07.320
by how much we have amended it?

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.600
- Such a great question.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.350
I mean, we can tell something about that

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:13.610
from the debate over whether
to have a bill of rights

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:14.620
in the first place.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:17.480
So James Madison, who's the
main author of the Constitution

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:19.430
originally opposes a bill of rights

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:22.130
cause he thinks it's
unnecessary or dangerous.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:23.110
It's unnecessary he says,

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.530
cause the Constitution
itself is a bill of rights,

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.310
since it only grants
Congress limited powers,

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:29.670
therefore Congress can't claim it

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:31.960
as the power to abridge
free speech, for example,

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.730
because it's not granted that power.

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:34.850
And then he says it's dangerous

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:36.250
to have a bill of rights because

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:38.220
if you have a list of rights,

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:39.480
people might wrongly think that

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:42.120
if a right isn't written
down, it's not protected.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.000
And he thought channeling The
Declaration of Independence,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.020
that our rights come from God
or nature not from government.

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:50.390
And therefore you can't make a close list.

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:51.280
There are so many of them

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:53.190
you couldn't even begin
to write them all down,

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.110
but then the anti-federalists

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:56.960
persuaded him to change his mind.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.560
They basically said
better safe than sorry.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.000
All of the state Constitutions
have bills of rights

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.200
since everyone knows
what the rights are let's

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:05.140
write down the main ones which they did,

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:07.160
adding the ninth Amendment which said

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:08.560
the enumeration of certain rights

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.150
shall not be construed
to deny or disparage

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.550
others retained by the people.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:14.240
That's a reminder that don't assume

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.540
if it's not written
down it's not protected.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:17.900
You know, as to the question of

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:19.300
did they expect it to change more?

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.320
Will the be surprised that

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.620
there there've been only 27 Amendments?

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.140
There was definitely a
variation of views on this.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.530
Jefferson who was not at the
Constitutional convention.

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:29.170
He was in Paris at the time

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:30.550
thought there should be essentially

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:32.760
a new convention every generation.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.110
And you had to allow each
generation to go back

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:38.373
to first principles and
draft a new Constitution

00:13:38.373 --> 00:13:39.560
and it would be great to have

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:41.880
lots of Amendments or even
to start from scratch.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.730
Madison completely disagreed.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:44.790
He was at the convention.

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.330
He thought it was basically a miracle

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.290
that this group of fallible

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:50.000
human beings were able to come to

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.170
the agreement that they did.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:53.740
And it would be really
dangerous to roll the Dyson,

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.420
open things up to another convention.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:59.030
So he was much more reluctant
to have more Amendments.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:01.880
And he wanted us always to
be guided by what he called

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:03.470
reason rather than passion.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.740
He wanted a very slow
and deliberate process

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:07.420
for Constitutional change.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:09.710
And perhaps he would not be dissatisfied.

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:11.480
There have been relatively few Amendments.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:12.940
So that was some of the debate.

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:15.451
But, it's remarkable, isn't it?

00:14:15.451 --> 00:14:17.200
That there have been only 27 Amendments

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:18.190
in all of American history.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.380
Those first 10 are the bill of rights.

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.350
And each of them is worth studying.

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:27.873
One final thing I'll say
is this evening at 6:30 PM,

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:31.750
live free on constitutioncenter.org.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.350
We are giving our Liberty Medal,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:37.290
the 2020 Liberty medal to
Justice Ruth Baden Ginsburg.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.890
And it's an incredibly inspiring series

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:42.680
of video tributes from
her favorite Opera singers

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.860
and special friends talking about

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:47.710
all that she's done for the Constitution.

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:51.180
And she has a very memorable vision.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:54.590
She says the Constitution has
become ever more embracive.

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.930
And when I asked her once
what she meant by that word,

00:14:56.930 --> 00:15:00.150
she's meant embracing the left out people,

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:03.160
not just grudgingly but with open arms.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:04.980
So in Justice Ginsburg's vision,

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.640
the Constitution was
intended by its framers

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.680
to be increasingly embracive.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:12.000
So the promise of The
Declaration of Independence

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.710
that all men are created
equal could be extended

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:18.990
to all men and women to people
of all races, creeds, colors,

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:21.300
to immigrants, to LGBTQ people.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.100
She believes that that
more embracive Constitution

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:25.450
was envisioned by the framers.

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:27.073
Now, others disagree.

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.800
People who are considered themselves

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.110
Constitutional originalists

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:35.040
believe that the Constitution
is only supposed to evolve

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:36.760
through the Amendment process

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.770
and any efforts to expand its protections

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:41.840
through judicial interpretation

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.200
are a violation of the
original understanding

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.110
of the Constitution.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.560
So that debate broadly
between call them originalists

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.540
or strict constructionist
and living Constitutionalists

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:54.580
is very much alive on
the Supreme Court today.

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:57.090
And it's great just to study
the different methodologies

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:58.700
of Constitutional interpretation.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:00.430
So you friends who were watching

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.730
can make up your own mind
about who you agree with.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:04.190
Are you a originalist?

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:05.460
Are you in a living Constitutionalist?

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:06.870
Would you mix and match

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:11.380
different methodologies like
pragmatism or representation,

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:13.070
reinforcement or textualism?

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:14.930
We can kind of walk out by

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:15.920
thinking like a justice

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:17.660
and talking about those
different approaches,

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:19.570
but it's always really inspiring

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.270
to try to approach these questions,

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:23.810
not in political but in
Constitutional terms.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:25.530
In other words, don't ask what
do you think the government

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.320
should do in a particular case,

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.130
but what do you think
the Constitution allows

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:30.047
or forbids it to do

00:16:30.047 --> 00:16:32.500
and be guided in your
answer to that question

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.470
by being faithful to your methodology.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:36.510
So if you're a originalist in one case,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:37.890
you should be that in another case,

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:39.980
even if it leads to a
result you don't like

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:42.120
and vice versa for living competition.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:45.550
So that's some of our
evangelism is to excite

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.290
all of you students who are watching

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:49.200
to think like a Constitutional lawyer,

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.800
learn those methodologies
and make up your own mind.

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:53.890
- That was fascinating.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:55.680
What would you say?

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.390
You know, it does feel like
earlier on in our history,

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:00.320
we had more frequent Amendments

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:03.940
and for very good reason
we had some major issues

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:07.410
in our early phases of our
country around slavery,

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.850
around women not having the right to vote.

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:11.600
And obviously even though
the Constitution got amended,

00:17:12.569 --> 00:17:14.650
some of many of the inequities,
many of the problems

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:16.530
have continued and to some degree

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:18.540
some of them are still there.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:19.700
What do you think?

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.660
And to some degree maybe
this more interpreted version

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:25.660
versus an originalist
version of the Constitution

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.840
maybe makes the Amendment process

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:32.460
a little bit less of a
must have is one theory,

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:34.210
but I'm curious, you know,

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:36.560
well we have a question actually

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:40.490
from India Hussein Akir says,
it's a lawyer from India

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:42.690
enlighten us regarding
the procedure of Amendment

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.120
of the Constitution of the U.S

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.620
and the reason why I'd love
you to answer that question

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:49.760
is because my perception
it's a very hard thing to do

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.820
and my second or third
part of this question

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:56.300
is what do you think is in
the running for Amendment?

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:58.180
I mean, you know, we think, we
hear a lot of people talking

00:17:58.180 --> 00:17:59.260
about the electoral college

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.040
or is, you know, every
state getting a senator

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.460
only two senators,
California has two senators

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:05.990
while the next 40 million people have,

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:07.770
you know, 40 senators or whatever.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:10.270
What do you think is in the running

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:11.800
and maybe our lifetimes that might,

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:13.960
where we might see an Amendment.

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.750
- Great question from India,

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:18.950
India has such a distinguished
Constitutional condition

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:22.450
And your Supreme Court is
really one of the great

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:25.830
and really influential

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.150
and important Supreme
Courts around the world.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:29.770
So how do you amend the U.S Constitution?

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.520
Well, there are a bunch of ways to do it,

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:33.470
according to article
five of the Constitution,

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:35.390
which sets out the Amendment process.

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:37.520
First, the Amendment has to be proposed.

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:38.940
There are two ways it can be proposed.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.730
It can either be proposed by two thirds

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:43.860
of both Houses of Congress

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.950
or by a special Constitutional convention

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:48.000
called by congress

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.070
on the application of
two thirds of the states.

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:53.610
Most of the Amendments that
have been proposed have used

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:56.450
the first method of proposal by Congress.

00:18:56.450 --> 00:18:58.220
We haven't had another
Constitutional convention

00:18:58.220 --> 00:18:59.730
since the first one.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:02.600
And then it can be ratified
in two ways as well.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.790
It can be either ratified
by three quarters

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:09.340
of the State Legislatures
or by special conventions

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:12.470
called in three quarters of the States.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:15.710
So that's why the Constitution
is so hard to amend

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:17.580
because you got to jump
through all of those hoops

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:20.030
and three quarters is a really high bar.

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:22.530
What Amendments are most likely?

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:25.810
The one that's very much on the table

00:19:25.810 --> 00:19:28.260
is the Equal Rights Amendment.

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:31.640
Just last year Virginia ratified
the Equal Rights Amendment

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:35.997
year after its proposal in
the 1980s or early 1980s.

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:45.283
And some claim that because
Virginia represents basically,

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:49.060
puts us over the three quarters hump,

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:50.890
the ERA should be part
of the Constitution.

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:53.470
But others disagree
because the original ERA

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.770
had a time limit and said
that it had to be ratified

00:19:55.770 --> 00:20:00.320
by the 1980s and the
time limit has passed.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:02.900
And therefore, in addition
to the fact that some States

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.170
have rescinded their ratification,

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:07.090
people say it is not valid.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.470
Justice Ginsburg, who is
one of the most influential

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.120
advocates of Equal Rights Amendment

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:13.120
thinks it would be safer
to start from scratch.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:16.146
Which if there's enough doubt
about the validity of the ERA,

00:20:16.146 --> 00:20:18.100
It'd be better to re-propose
it and re ratify it

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:19.850
because you wanna be sure that it's

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:21.630
definitely part of the Constitution.

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:24.210
And then in terms of what
other Amendments are likely,

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:28.490
surprisingly the Amendment that
has been proposed most often

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.830
and ratified by most States is,

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:33.755
I wanna ask you to guess,

00:20:33.755 --> 00:20:34.730
because it wasn't intuitive to me

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:36.400
but it's The Balanced Budget Amendment,

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:39.800
which has gotten nearly
two thirds of the States.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:43.120
I think it's like three
or four States short

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:45.860
of the three quarters benchmark.

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:48.600
So that's probably the first
one likely to be ratified

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.040
if that were actually to happen.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:51.807
Then there's also been
a term limits Amendment

00:20:51.807 --> 00:20:54.550
that's not nearly as
close to being ratified.

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:57.223
And those two are at the top of the list.

00:20:58.070 --> 00:20:58.903
- So just to make sure I understand.

00:20:58.903 --> 00:21:00.010
So The Balance Budget Amendment,

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:01.270
I actually was not aware of this.

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:03.310
I mean, they should be like
tracking this in the news

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:04.770
that would force a balanced budget

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:06.390
on the federal government?

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.240
- Exactly, right.

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:10.450
- So that would be a huge thing
because then all of a sudden

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:14.030
Fiscal policy is a tool
becomes significantly stopped,

00:21:16.232 --> 00:21:17.065
(Chuckling)

00:21:17.065 --> 00:21:18.590
I guess, especially when running deficit.

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.970
- Imagine in the time of
COVID given the deficits

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:24.030
we're running, all of that
with the unconstitutional

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.420
if we had a balanced budget Amendments

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:27.323
so we have a huge time period.

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:30.460
- Fascinating, once the state ratifies it,

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:31.470
can they undo their ratification

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:34.490
or they have to vote again
to undo the ratification.

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:35.410
- They have to vote again and it's obvious

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:37.260
really interesting legal questions about

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:38.650
whether you're allowed to or not.

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:41.690
Several States did undo or
resend their ratifications

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:44.420
of ERA and there's a
debate among lawyers about

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:47.803
whether those decisions are valid or not.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:52.800
In the end it's sort of up to,

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:54.710
well, the last contestant Amendment we had

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.100
was the 27th Amendment.

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:59.470
Was originally proposed as
the original second Amendment

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:01.440
back at the time of the bill of rights

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.760
and it says that Congress
can't raise its salary

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:05.530
without an intervening election.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:07.487
It had got a bunch of States to ratify it

00:22:07.487 --> 00:22:08.930
and then people forgot about it.

00:22:08.930 --> 00:22:13.930
And then in the 1990s, I
think, a college student said,

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:17.660
hey, we're two States
short of ratification or so

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:18.930
just a few more ratifications

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:20.410
since there's no time limit here

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:22.150
would make this part of the Constitution.

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:24.970
He got a failing grade on the paper

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:26.920
cause his teacher thought
it was so outlandish,

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.110
but a bunch of States
took up a suggestion,

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:31.287
ratified the Amendment
and the arc of this,

00:22:31.287 --> 00:22:34.000
the United States decided on his own

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:35.620
that it was part of the Constitution

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:37.480
after the final ratification came through

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:38.440
and certified it.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:41.110
And it was then inscribed
in the Constitution

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:42.730
as the 27th Amendment.

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:45.870
And the story Justice Gorsuch
told this the noon today.

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.380
He thinks that the guy
had his grade reversed

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:49.810
after the Amendment was ratified.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:51.270
But it's a great example of how,

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:54.210
when there's no time
limit on an Amendment,

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:57.850
it's kind of up to well in
that case, just the archivist

00:22:57.850 --> 00:22:59.510
and then people's willingness to accept it

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:02.200
about whether or not it's valid
in the question of precision

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:04.070
is equally interesting.

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:05.630
- That's incredible, that's a facet.

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:06.644
When did this happen?

00:23:06.644 --> 00:23:09.121
(indistinct)

00:23:09.121 --> 00:23:10.410
- Well, I think it became,

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.729
I can pull out the
interactive Constitution.

00:23:12.729 --> 00:23:13.930
You guys will figure it out,

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:15.170
but it was in the early 1990s

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:17.050
when the 27th Equal Rights Amendments was.

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:19.915
- Wow! And the Equal Rights Amendments,

00:23:19.915 --> 00:23:20.783
you know, I've watched the,

00:23:20.783 --> 00:23:21.958
